<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yahdun-Lim</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1800 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century-bce">19th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yahdun-Lim was an Amorite king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> around 1800 BCE. He subdued the pastoral tribes of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, founded fortresses and led an expedition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, where, according to his foundation inscription, he washed his weapons in the sea. He was murdered in a palace coup, Mari soon fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad I</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a> later claimed descent from him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yahdun-lim.jpg" alt="Yahdun-Lim" width="320" height="290" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Disk_Yahdun-Lim_Louvre_AO18236.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yahdun-Lim, whose name appears in Akkadian as ia-aḫ-du-un-li-im and in Amorite as Yaʿdun-lîm, was of Amorite origin and became king after the death of his father, Yaggid-Lim. He styled himself king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, Tuttul and the Land of Hana, and under him Mari became one of the major powers of the region in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-bronze-age">Middle Bronze Age</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He began his reign by subduing seven rebelling tribal leaders and rebuilding the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terqa">Terqa</a>, and he built a new fort named after himself, Dur-Yahdun-Lim. Nomad tribes threatened the kingdom, but he made them submit and pay tribute, and once internal peace was secured he built a temple to the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utu">Shamash</a>. He then expanded westward and claimed to have reached the coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean Sea</a>.</p><p>In the east he was allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a>. That alliance threatened the interests of Sumu-Epuh, king of Yamhad, who backed a rebellion of the Yaminite nomads centred on Tuttul. Yahdun-Lim defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaminites">Yaminites</a>, but an open war with Yamhad was avoided. In the north-east he was drawn into a rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad I</a> of Shubat-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a>, whose expansion led other threatened kings to ask Yahdun-Lim for help.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Before he could move against Shamshi-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Adad</a>, Yahdun-Lim was assassinated, possibly by his own son Sumu-Yamam, who was himself killed two years after taking the throne. In the chaos that followed Shamshi-Adad advanced and annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> and gave its lordship to his son Yasmah-Adad, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, where he remained in exile until Shamshi-Adad's death.</p><p>An inscribed cone found in the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> and now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> records his works and the founding of Dur-Yahdun-Lim. In it the king says that the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagan</a> proclaimed his kingship, that he took prisoner seven kings who had fought against him, opened canals, built the wall of Mari and dug its moat, and founded a city in a parched land where no king had built before.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yahdun-Lim” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the early second millennium <b>Yahdun-Lim</b> of the Lim dynasty fought the tribes of the steppe and neighbouring kings, and led an expedition to the Mediterranean, where he claimed to have washed his weapons in the sea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mari">Mari · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 18th century BCE Tuttul was a seat of Amorite tribal kings who fought <b>Yahdun-Lim</b> of Mari, and later fell under Shamshi-Adad and Zimri-Lim.</blockquote><a href="/state/tuttul">Tuttul</a></li></ul></section>
